<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our New Classmates</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Where are you from?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a personal diary entry about a recent event. Interactive: Reply to a message written on paper, in a text app, or in an email.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a personal diary entry about a recent event at school or in the community (e.g., "Today, I joined a new class. I felt nervous at first, but then I met Juan Manuel, and we became friends.").
                Can reply to a simple message from a classmate about school activities (e.g., "Hi Tammy! Yes, I will bring my notebook for the science project tomorrow. See you in class!").
                Can describe a recent move from one school to another in a short email to a friend (e.g., "Hello, Bill. This is Juan Carlos. Recently, I moved to a new school in Chiriqui. It is bigger than my old school in Puerto Armueles. It is more modern, quieter, and cleaner. I will make new friends here!").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students will complete a "Quick Write" activity. The teacher displays a picture of a new student looking nervous on their first day at a new school. Students write 2-3 sentences describing how the student might feel and what they might do. They can use words like 'nervous', 'new', 'friend', 'talk', 'learn', 'play', 'study'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the writing process by thinking aloud and writing 2-3 sentences on the board about the picture. For example: "This new classmate looks nervous. I think he will join a new class today. He hopes to make a new friend." The teacher explicitly uses and highlights some of the target vocabulary and present/future tense. The teacher also models the pronunciation of /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ endings for plurals (classmates, friends) and third-person singular verbs (looks, hopes, joins) as they are written and read aloud.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main topic of our short writing today? (Is it about animals or new students?)" – Expected answer: New students.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we write about what happened yesterday or what might happen today/soon for a new student? (Past or Present/Future?)" – Expected answer: Present/Future (and perhaps past feelings).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our New Classmates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents an authentic-looking short email written by a 7th-grade student named Mariana to her friend, describing her recent move to a new school. The email integrates vocabulary such as 'new school', 'old school', 'classmate', 'nervous', 'excited', 'bigger', 'more modern', 'cleaner', 'used to walk', 'takes the bus', 'will make new friends'. It also uses Past Simple, Present Simple, Comparatives, and Future Tense.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying the main ideas and specific details in a written message about personal experiences and school environments.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the email individually.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the email mainly about? (a new pet or a new school?)" "Who wrote the email?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match phrases about Mariana's old school to her new school (e.g., "was small" -> "old school"; "is bigger" -> "new school").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Mariana felt excited when she joined the new school." "Her new school is older than her previous school."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing descriptive sentences and paragraphs using Present and Past Simple for habits and descriptions, Comparatives and Superlatives, and Future tense. Reinforcing vocabulary related to school and community.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentences from the example email with missing words (vocabulary words like 'nervous', 'friendly', 'bigger', 'used to', 'will make'). Students complete the sentences individually, focusing on correct word choice and grammar. Example: "My new school is ______ (big) than my old one." "I _______ (walk) to school, but now I take the bus."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Paragraph Writing:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides a template or sentence starters for describing a new classmate or a recent event in their school/community. For example: "I have a new classmate named ______. He/She is from ______. He/She is ______ (adjective, e.g., friendly, quiet). His/Her previous school ______ (past simple description). Our school is ______ (comparative adjective) than his/her old school. I think he/she will ______ (future action)." Students write a short paragraph together.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Check/Proofreading:</b> Pairs exchange their written paragraphs with another pair. They check for correct use of Past/Present Simple and Comparatives/Superlatives, target vocabulary, and general clarity. The teacher circulates, offering guidance on writing structure and grammar. Pronunciation of plural endings and third-person verbs can be briefly discussed if students read their sentences aloud during correction (e.g., "Does 'schools' have an /s/ or /z/ sound? Does 'studies' end in /z/ or /ɪz/?").</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Writing – Reply to a message or write a diary entry.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students will choose one of two writing tasks:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option A (Reply to a Message):</b> Students imagine they received a simple message from a classmate (provided by the teacher) asking about their preferences for a school activity or comparing two subjects. They will write a short reply (3-5 sentences) on paper or simulating a text app. The reply must use comparatives/superlatives and future tense. Example message: "Hi! Which is better, science or math project? When will you bring your materials?" Student reply: "Hi! I think the science project is more interesting. I will bring my notebook tomorrow. See you!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option B (Diary Entry):</b> Students write a short personal diary entry (5-7 sentences) about a recent event at school or in the community related to meeting a new classmate or experiencing a new environment. They must include at least two sentences using Past Simple, two using Present Simple, and one using Comparatives or Future tense. They should use vocabulary like 'nervous', 'excited', 'joined', 'met', 'friendly', 'big', 'small', 'previous'. Example: "Today, I joined a new club activity. I was a little nervous at first. But then I met a new classmate, Sofia. She is very friendly. My old club was smaller, but this one is more exciting. I will definitely participate next week!"</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reminds students to present personal and classmate information using appropriate language, ensuring clarity and coherence in their written output. They should focus on expressing their ideas clearly and making sure their message is easy to understand.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and Peer Assessment. Students will exchange their final written product with a partner for a final peer check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will hand in their written reply or diary entry.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Did the student address the prompt (reply to message/diary entry)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., classmate, nervous, friendly, community).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of Past Simple, Present Simple, Comparatives/Superlatives, and Future tense.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Coherence:</b> Is the message easy to understand? Are sentences well-structured?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or grammar point I used in my writing today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I still find challenging about writing in English is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now about writing a short paragraph about a new experience or classmate?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one tip I would give to a friend who wants to improve their English writing skills?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., specific activity, student engagement, clarity of instructions)." The structured practice activities helped students build confidence before the production task. The modeling and clear examples were effective for this group.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next lesson to improve student writing, especially regarding grammar or vocabulary usage?" Some students struggled with differentiating between Present Simple and Past Simple, and some comparative forms. Next time, I will prepare an additional mini-activity focusing solely on comparative adjectives. I will also provide a checklist for grammar points during the peer-check phase.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The written pieces from today's lesson will serve as input for Lesson 5, where students will mediate and integrate information by summarizing or presenting their written experiences to others, connecting their personal stories to a wider project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>